From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 10/21] dndcp: Copy and paste through the Xwayland selection on
 GNOME

On GNOME wl-copy and wl-paste each map a window to be given focus, and
it shows in the dock and takes the focus for a moment on every run. The
host asks for the guest clipboard every few seconds while the pointer
crosses the VM's edge, so the window keeps flashing.

Mutter keeps the X CLIPBOARD selection in step with the Wayland one, and
answers any X client for it, focused or not. Where there is no
ext-data-control-v1 but there is an X display, the backend now owns and
reads that selection instead. Host clips go on it through GDK, with
every format the host sent, and Mutter makes them the Wayland selection.
Reads use a separate Xlib connection with an unmapped InputOnly window,
and a property of their own for each read, so that a late reply to an
earlier read is ignored. Large selections arrive through INCR, up to
CPCLIPITEM_MAX_SIZE_V3. wl-copy and wl-paste remain for a session
without an X display, and wl-paste also reads when the X selection has
no owner.

GDK on X11 reports each change of owner of the X selection, as "changed"
in GTK4 and as "owner-change" in GTK3, without reading anything, and
Mutter sets the owner again on every change of the Wayland selection.
The backend counts those notifications and reads the selection only
after one, as it does with the events of data control. A selection that
this process owns through GDK is not read back. GDK would answer such a
read from the main loop, which the read holds, so the read would only
wait out its budget.

Tested by hand with the whole series on GNOME 50.3 (Bluefin 44), where
text went both ways through the X selection.
